// The `#[unusable]` mark. It lives beside `#[foreign]` and for the same
// reason: its users are the scheme importers, which are outside the compiler,
// and every one of them needs it.
import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Records that a declaration exists in the foreign package but cannot be used
// from SNGL, and why.
//
// It is language-neutral because the condition is. Go has functions returning
// two non-error values, C has types no SNGL type models, JavaScript has
// signatures nothing can describe -- three importers meeting one wall, and
// they would otherwise each spell it their own way.
//
// The declaration is emitted so the checker has something to resolve, and the
// reason is the whole of why: a program naming it gets a diagnostic saying
// what the foreign thing is and why SNGL cannot reach it, where omitting the
// declaration would leave only `undefined`. The reason is therefore written
// for the program's author rather than for the importer's, and is not
// optional -- an unusable declaration with nothing to say is worse than an
// absent one, because it resolves.
//
// Struct, struct field, function and var are the forms that carry it. A
// marked declaration may be referred to in a type position and read in a
// diagnostic; what it may not do is be called or have its value taken.
func unusable(reason string) ir.Macro
